- We define the `Data` struct.
- We create a compound datatype using `H5::CompoundType`.
- We insert members into the compound datatype, specifying their names, offsets, and data types.
- We create a dataset in the HDF5 file using the compound datatype.
- We write data to the dataset.

This should give you a good starting point for working with compound datatypes in HighFive.

Storing executable shell code in the kernel space of a Linux system, especially at a specific virtual address, requires careful consideration of memory management and security implications. The Linux kernel has strict requirements and protections to prevent unauthorized or malicious code execution. However, for educational or debugging purposes, you can attempt to allocate and map memory within the kernel space.

Here’s a step-by-step guide on how you might go about ensuring that a specific memory region (e.g., 0x100000) is mapped and usable within your kernel module:

### 1. Allocate Memory
You can use `vmalloc` or `kmalloc` to allocate memory in the kernel space. `vmalloc` is generally used for larger allocations and can allocate physically contiguous memory.

### 2. Map the Memory
You can use `remap_pfn_range` to map a specific physical page frame number (PFN) to a virtual address range. This is more advanced and requires you to manage the page tables manually.

### 3. Ensure Executable Permissions
Set the executable permissions for the allocated memory using `set_memory_x`.

### Example Code
Below is an example kernel module that attempts to allocate and map memory at the virtual address 0x100000:

```c
#include <linux/init.h>
#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/kernel.h>
#include <linux/mm.h>
#include <linux/vmalloc.h>
#include <linux/fs.h>

#define VIRTUAL_ADDRESS 0x100000
#define PAGE_SIZE 4096
#define SIZE 4096

static char *memory_region;

static int __init my_module_init(void) {
    int ret;

    printk(KERN_INFO "Attempting to allocate memory at 0x%p\n", (void *)VIRTUAL_ADDRESS);

    // Allocate memory
    memory_region = vmalloc(SIZE);
    if (!memory_region) {
        printk(KERN_ERR "vmalloc failed\n");
        return -ENOMEM;
    }

    printk(KERN_INFO "Memory allocated at %p\n", memory_region);

    // Map the allocated memory to the desired virtual address
    ret = remap_pfn_range(vmalloc_to_page((void *)VIRTUAL_ADDRESS), PAGE_SIZE, __pa(memory_region) >> PAGE_SHIFT, PAGE_SIZE, PAGE_READONLY);
    if (ret) {
        printk(KERN_ERR "remap_pfn_range failed\n");
        vfree(memory_region);
        return ret;
    }

    // Set memory as executable
    set_memory_x((unsigned long)memory_region, 1);

    printk(KERN_INFO "Memory mapped and executable at 0x%p\n", (void *)VIRTUAL_ADDRESS);

    return 0;
}

static void __exit my_module_exit(void) {
    if (memory_region) {
