```sas
/* Create the new dataset without column A */
data vertical2;
    set vertical3;
    drop Treatment; /* Dropping column A */
    format Effectiveness $Effective., Sex $Sex.;
run;
```

In this code:
- `data vertical2;` creates a new dataset named `vertical2`.
- `set vertical3;` reads the data from the dataset `vertical3`.
- `drop Treatment;` removes the column `Treatment` (which you mentioned as column A).
- `format Effectiveness $Effective., Sex $Sex.;` applies the specified formats to the remaining columns `Effectiveness` and `Sex`.

If `Treatment` is not the name of column A but you want to drop the first column based on its position, you can use the `DROP` statement with the `_ALL_` option to drop all columns except the ones you specify. However, since SAS requires column names or variables to be specified for dropping, you need to know the exact variable name.

If you don't know the exact names of the columns and want to drop the first column based on its position, you might need to use a more complex approach, such as reading the column names and then dropping the first one dynamically. Here’s an example of how you can do that:

```sas
/* Read the variable names */
proc contents data=vertical3 out=vars(noprint);
